<BaseLayout title="Reception — Astrological Lexicon">
  <div class="shell page-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <p class="eyebrow">Planetary relation · Draft entry</p>
      <h1>Reception</h1>
      <p class="lede">A relationship in which one planet is received through a dignity belonging to another. Historical authors do not formulate the doctrine in exactly the same way, so the entry keeps each source account separate.</p>

      <div class="mode-switch" aria-label="Display mode">
        <button class="active" data-mode="reading">Reading</button>
        <button data-mode="sources">Sources</button>
      </div>

      <section id="definition">
        <h2>Working definition</h2>
        <p>Reception concerns the relation created when a planet occupies a place that belongs, by dignity, to another planet. Mutual reception occurs when that relationship is reciprocal. The strength and practical consequences of reception vary by author and by the dignity involved.</p>
        <p class="sources-only meta">Editorial status: draft synthesis. This paragraph is intentionally broader than any single source definition and must remain tied to the source accounts below.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="biruni">
        <h2>al-Bīrūnī</h2>
        <div class="source-block">
          <div class="source-head">
            <h3>Book of Instruction</h3>
            <span class="source-label">§507 · pp. 330–331</span>
          </div>
          <p>al-Bīrūnī describes reception through a planet arriving in a dignity proper to another planet. If the second planet is likewise in a place proper to the first, the relationship becomes mutual reception. He adds that the condition is strengthened when the relevant places are richer in dignities and when the aspect does not indicate hostility.</p>
          <p class="source-cite">R. Ramsay Wright translation, §507.</p>
          <div class="sources-only">
            <p class="meta">Evidence type: direct source definition. Public wording: paraphrase. Related section §508 immediately continues with application and donor terminology.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="lilly">
        <h2>William Lilly</h2>
        <div class="source-block">
          <div class="source-head">
            <h3>Christian Astrology</h3>
            <span class="source-label">Book I · p. 112</span>
          </div>
          <p>Lilly defines reception between significators through their being in one another's dignities. He explicitly allows reception by house, triplicity, term, face, or another essential dignity, while treating reception by house as the strongest form in the examples given there.</p>
          <p>For horary judgment he gives mutual reception substantial practical weight, including cases where the ordinary aspectual testimony is difficult or absent.</p>
          <p class="source-cite">William Lilly, <em>Christian Astrology</em>, Book I, p. 112 in the digital retype used by this project.</p>
          <div class="sources-only">
            <p class="meta">Evidence type: direct source definition. Compare Lilly's dignity table, where mutual reception by house is given the same score as domicile and reception by exaltation the same score as exaltation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="variation">
        <h2>Variation between authors</h2>
        <p>The current corpus already shows why reception should not be reduced to a single modern sentence. al-Bīrūnī explains the relationship through the receiving planet's dignities and emphasizes mutuality and qualitative strengthening. Lilly presents a practical hierarchy of receptions and uses the doctrine directly in horary perfection.</p>
        <p class="sources-only meta">This comparison is provisional until Bonatti, Antiochus, and Abū Maʿshar passages have been verified and added.</p>
      </section>

